Despite extensive research on misinformation dissemination through social media, the underlying mechanisms of online conversational networks remain poorly understood. Furthermore, the influence of user interdependence in shaping the acceptance or rejection of misleading information remains a complex area of study. We conducted a study on Sina Weibo users discussing COVID-19 vaccines, collecting a dataset of 578K user interactions. By analyzing Twitter dialogue, we distinguished three types of online conversational networks: central, peripheral, and interdependent. We found that users in interdependent conversations were more likely to engage with anti-vaccination tweets, leading to increased vaccination skepticism. Our study reveals how online conversational structures influence user attitudes toward misleading vaccine information, offering significant implications for social media platforms and public health communication campaigns. 

\textbf{Keywords:} social media, misinformation, user networks, dialogue analysis, behavior change, natural language, machine learning

