This study verifies that the helicity functional of the compressible Euler equations remains constant over time, utilizing an approach based on functional calculus and the properties of Lagrangian perturbations. Building on earlier research regarding conservation of momentum and energy, this work relies on basic properties of Lagrangian perturbations and the Cauchy-Green strain tensor. No additional assumptions about smoothness or specific properties of the solution are required. Furthermore, this study demonstrates that helicity is consistently conserved in both barotropic and polytropic flows. Results are illustrated using two-dimensional numerical examples in both ideal barotropic and polytropic flows. Research findings also suggest that helicity might be a relevant and useful property in the study of three-dimensional compressible flows. Moreover, this study paves the way for investigation of other conserved properties in fluids. 

