Quantum order parameters in systems with low-dimensional lattices often face competition from multiple orders, typically leading to randomized phase diagrams. Introducing a structural distortion into the triangular lattice Hubbard model, however, yields a cleaner phase diagram, with multiple orders merging into a single framework and giving rise to a generalized phase diagram. Observations of double-humped curves in plots of magnetic chemical potential versus filling indicate the interplay of two different types of magnetic orders, where adjustments for zero-point fluctuations eliminate the double hump, resulting in a more intuitive, straightforward phase diagram. Notably, this correction mirrors the effects seen on hexagonal lattice models where a similar structural distortion induces a similar phase diagram, suggesting a new concept, 'order-by-disorder,' where neighboring orders compete to the point of instability, giving rise to a new structural state. This new idea can be applied not only to Hubbard models but also to other triangular-lattice models with weakly coupled spins. Lastly, our research indicates that certain states, like the antiferromagnetic nematic state, might actually be stable at filling one and potentially viable in materials like Y$_2$Ru$_2$O$_7$. 	extbf{Editor's note:} To view the complete study in an animated format, visit the link to the research data at https://dx.doi.org/10.6082/M1DW2WJC.} 
